          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding contrast between her heart and her limited speech reinforces the utterance as a candid avowal, regardless of whether its sincerity could be independently verified.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The immediate modifier “According to my bond” makes this occurrence an affirmation and invocation of Cordelia’s existing filial obligation, not merely a report that such an obligation exists. Her subsequent explanation identifies that bond with returning the duties owed to the father who begot, raised, and loved her. It is not a new promise with newly specified future terms, but it positions her as presently bound to love, obey, and honour Lear.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"Although Cordelia speaks under pressure and calls herself unhappy, the focal expression is a controlled, propositional answer qualified by the measured formula “According to my bond; no more nor less.” It is not represented as a reflex-like affective outburst or expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.83,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming a filial bond, the occurrence has a moderately supported boundary-setting and corrective function. Cordelia answers Lear’s competitive demand while refusing the unlimited, hyperbolic formulation he seeks; “no more nor less” defines the proper extent of her claim rather than trying to maximize it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare how much they love him, with a richer territorial share tied to the answer. After initially saying “Nothing,” Cordelia gives the focal avowal as a restrained answer, grounding her love in her filial bond rather than competing through extravagance.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The expression affirms an existing filial undertaking: Cordelia positions herself as bound to return fitting duties to Lear through obedience, love, and honour. Its terms remain general rather than constituting a new, specific promise. Lear does not accept it as an adequate response to his demand; he tells her to “Mend your speech a little,” so the affirmation receives no satisfactory uptake from him in the supplied passage.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, Scene I. No omitted plot material has been used in the assessment.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents Cordelia as claiming genuine affection, including through her asides, but it cannot independently prove her inward sincerity. The supplied evidence also does not establish any later fulfilment or breach of the affirmed duties, and none is assumed.\"}"
